<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01HC">Remark <span data-tag="01HC">4.5.1.21</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is a Kan complex, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a Kan complex. To prove this, it suffices to show that every morphism $u: X \rightarrow Y$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an isomorphism (Proposition <a data-tag="019D" href="/tag/019D">4.4.2.1</a>). By virtue of Remark <a data-tag="01HB" href="/tag/01HB">4.5.1.20</a>, this is equivalent to the assertion that $F(u): F(X) \rightarrow F(Y)$ is an isomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, which is automatic when $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is a Kan complex (Proposition <a data-tag="0052" href="/tag/0052">1.4.6.10</a>). Similarly, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a Kan complex, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is a Kan complex (this follows by applying the same argument to an inverse equivalence $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$). </p>
